{"type":"text","timestamp":1790931825296,"sessionID":"ses_f04269f0effecb5uQj2TE3xP33","part":{"id":"prt_0fbdae87c001oQ1ajOeWaGU2ai","messageID":"msg_0fbdad27a001W25GB9MHHDMyG3","sessionID":"ses_f04269f0effecb5uQj2TE3xP33","type":"text","text":"The patch has been submitted. Summary of the change:\n\n**`src/blinker/base.py`** — `Namespace.signal` now validates its `name` argument with `isinstance(name, str)` as the first statement, raising `TypeError(\"name must be a string\")` before the dictionary lookup or any modification. This ensures:\n- Non-string names are rejected even when the namespace already contains that key (the check precedes `if name not in self`)\n- `str` subclasses and the empty string remain accepted\n- The module-level `signal` helper is covered too, since it's bound to `default_namespace.signal`\n- Signal identity for repeated names, the original docstrings, and receiver dispatch are all untouched\n\n**`CHANGES.rst`** — added a note under the unreleased 1.10.0 section.\n\nSince this environment has no execution tools, the behavior was verified by inspection only; I noted in the submission that the exact error message wording is my choice (any `TypeError` meets the stated requirement).","time":{"start":1790931822716,"end":1790931825290}}}
